```java
public interface EventBus {
    <T> void publish(T event);
    <T> void subscribe(Class<T> eventType, Consumer<T> handler);
}

public class InMemoryEventBus implements EventBus {
    private final Map<Class<?>, List<Consumer<?>>> handlers = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public <T> void subscribe(Class<T> type, Consumer<T> handler) {
        handlers.computeIfAbsent(type, k -> new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>()).add(handler);
    }

    public <T> void publish(T event) {
        for (Consumer<?> h : handlers.getOrDefault(event.getClass(), List.of())) {
            ((Consumer<T>) h).accept(event);
        }
    }
}
```

Keying by the event's class gives compile-time type safety on `subscribe` while keeping `publish` generic — a publisher never needs to know who, if anyone, is listening.
